---
alwaysApply: true
---

1. Build iOS UI with SwiftUI primitives and shared design tokens in `mobile-ios/WayTwo/Core/Design/`. Do not hardcode repeated spacing, radius, typography, or color values; add and reuse typed tokens.
2. All user-facing copy must come from String Catalogs (`mobile-ios/WayTwo/Resources/Localizable.xcstrings`) with both `en` and `uk` translations added together.
3. Use structured logging through `mobile-ios/WayTwo/Core/Monitoring/AppLogger.swift`. Do not use `print()` for runtime logging. Never log secrets, access tokens, auth codes, deep-link payloads, or personal data.
4. Any async screen or flow must surface recoverable errors in UI and record non-sensitive diagnostics in logs.
5. Keep feature code organized by domain folders under `mobile-ios/WayTwo/Features/` with isolated reducers, actions, and views.
6. Prefer platform-native APIs over cross-platform abstractions for storage, networking reachability, navigation, haptics, and media access.
7. Before adding UI code that already exists or could be reused, check `mobile-ios/WayTwo/Core/UI/` for an existing component. If a UI pattern appears in two or more places, extract it into a shared component in `Core/UI/` rather than duplicating it. The same applies to logic: shared helpers and extensions belong in `Core/`, not copied into feature files.
8. Do not comment what a variable name, ZStack ordering, or array declaration already expresses. Leave comments only for non-obvious implementation techniques, Figma node-ID traceability, cross-platform token parity, or edge-case warnings. Descriptive prefixes that restate the identifier name are not acceptable; trailing Figma spec references (node IDs, pixel values) are.
9. All `AppColors` semantic tokens must adapt light/dark via `adaptive(light:dark:)` (or fixed `Color` only when both modes use the same value). Never use raw `Color.black` / `Color.white` for tokens that differ by appearance. Use a private `uiRGB(_:_:_:alpha:)` helper for sRGB components; do not scatter bare `/255` fraction arithmetic at callsites.
10. Name shared helpers, view modifiers, and Swift extensions for their behavior, not the flow or feature where they were first written. If a symbol is used outside its origin domain, its name must not carry a domain-specific prefix.
11. Any view or object that owns logic for more than one subdomain must split that logic into `extension TypeName` blocks in separate files named `TypeName+Subdomain.swift` (not `private extension` across files — Swift treats that as file-scoped). The primary file retains structure and navigation; domain side effects belong in the corresponding extension file. Apply this split as soon as a file covers more than one domain.
12. In `NavigationStack` flows, every screen must set `navigationTitle` so the back chevron and long-press back-history list always show a correct label — even when the bar is visually hidden with `toolbar(.hidden, for: .navigationBar)`. `navigationTitle` feeds the back-stack metadata independently of bar visibility. To suppress a duplicate on-screen headline, remove the body `Text` title, not the navigation bar; to keep a custom body title and hide the bar, pair `toolbar(.hidden, for: .navigationBar)` with `navigationTitle` so the back history still has a value.
13. Offline-first writes that must reach the server: persist locally, enqueue `Outbox` work via `ProfileSync` (or the domain sync client), then return so the UI can advance. Never call `Outbox.flush` from a write-path API. `SyncCoordinator` owns all flush triggers (app foreground, network restore to online, post-auth / session active). Log `log.info` at enqueue and flush boundaries with structured metadata (`op`, `status`, counts); never log tokens, payloads with PII, or raw URLs.
